              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.492,
            0.211,
            0.511
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.412,
            0.521,
            0.583,
            0.559
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_15_equation_11.jpg",
          "content": "\\frac{2}{3} \\div \\frac{3}{5} = \\frac{5}{3} \\times \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{10}{9}."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.573,
            0.288,
            0.593
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Discussion"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.605,
            0.846,
            0.644
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In each of the division problems above, observe how we found the answer. Can we frame a rule that tells us how to divide two fractions?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.643,
            0.534,
            0.661
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us consider the previous problem."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.661,
            0.546,
            0.736
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In every division problem we have a dividend, divisor and quotient. The technique we have been using to get the quotient is:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.739,
            0.545,
            0.814
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. \tFirst, find the number which gives 1 when multiplied by the divisor. We see that the resulting number is a fraction whose numerator is the"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.576,
            0.651,
            0.846,
            0.788
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 17,
              "bbox": [
                0.576,
                0.651,
                0.846,
                0.788
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_15_image_17.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.186,
            0.813,
            0.847,
            0.915
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "divisor’s denominator and denominator is the divisor’s numerator. For the divisor "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac{3}{5}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " this fraction is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "{ \\frac { 5 } { 3 } } ."
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " We call "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 5 } { 3 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " the reciprocal of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac{3}{5},"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " When we multiply a fraction by its reciprocal, we get 1. So, the first step in our technique is to find the divisor’s reciprocal."
            }
          ],
          "continues_prev": true
